<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<class name="NetwPredictionEngine" inherits="RefCounted" api_type="extension" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/master/doc/class.xsd">
	<brief_description>
		The session's prediction engines, one pool keyed by an opaque slot.
	</brief_description>
	<description>
		A predicting entity's engine has identity and memory, and there is one per predicting entity rather than one per session. The pool is the registered object and the engines are rows in it, because registering a class per entity would hand a caller something that has to dissolve into slot-keyed storage later.
		A slot decides and never performs. It reads the facts a caller hands it, answers with a plan, and writes nothing outside itself: no node, no clock, no binding. That is what lets a transition be reproduced from its journal row alone, and it is why the caller stays responsible for reading colliders, calling a body and writing values back.
		[codeblock]
		slot = open(declaration)          one slot per predicting entity
		configure(slot, ...)              refused outside what supports() admits
		record_input / open_drive         the pass a caller performs
		close_drive                       the row the pass produced
		admit_ack / compare_state         what the far peer answered
		recover / quarantine_state        the plan a caller then writes
		close(slot)                       the entity is gone
		[/codeblock]
		[method supports] is the flip axis. The pool declares which CONFIGURATIONS it can drive, a caller asks before handing it a tick, and outside the declared set [method configure] refuses rather than storing. There is never a mixed engine, so no per-region hop is paid at runtime.
		Every role is driven, because the pass a slot performs is the same one whichever peer owns the entity: a role decides whether a caller reaches the slot, never what the slot then does.
	</description>
